  <subsection xml:id="subsec-apportionment-terminology">
    <title>Apportionment Terminology</title>

    <p>
      One of the most famous apportionment problems is the apportionment of seats in the U.S.
      House of Representatives among the U.S.
      states.
      In fact, the terminology for apportionment problems comes from this application.
    </p>

    <definition>
      <title>Apportionment Terms</title>

      <p>
        <dl>
        <li>
          <title><term>Apportionment Problem</term></title>

          <p>
            a scenario in which <m>m</m> identical and <m>n</m> indivisible items (the <q>seats</q>) must be divided among <m>n</m> entities (the <q>states</q>) proportionately (using the <q>populations</q>)
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title><term>States</term></title>

          <p>
            the entities in the apportionment problem
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title><term>Seats</term></title>

          <p>
            the items being apportioned among the states
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title><term>Apportionment Method</term></title>

          <p>
            the algorithm determining how to apportion the <m>m</m> seats to the <m>n</m> states, guaranteeing exactly <m>m</m> seats are distributed proporitionaly to each of the <m>n</m> states based on their populations <m>p_s</m> for <m>1 \le s \le n</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title><term>Standard Divisor <m>D</m></term> and <term>Standard Quota <m>Q_s</m></term>, <term>Lower Quota<m>L_s</m></term>, <term>Upper Quota <m>U_s</m></term></title>

          <p>
            <m>D = p/m</m> where <m>p = \sum_{s = 1}^n p_s</m> is the total population; <br /> <m>Q_s = p_s/D</m>, representing the <q>fair</q> share of seats for state <m>s</m> if the seats could be subdivided; <br /> <m>L_s = \lfloor Q_s \rfloor</m>, the largest integer less than or equal to the standard quota; <m>U_s = L_s + 1</m>, the smallest integer strictly greater than the standard quota. <aside>The definition of the floor function varies from setting to setting. Here we take it to include the possibility of equality.</aside>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title><term>Modified Divisor (and friends)</term></title>

          <p>
            A value <m>d</m> or <m>D'</m> other than the standard divisor, used to make the distribution of seats produced by rounding (based on a given convention) add up to <m>m</m>.
            From <m>d</m> each state gets a modified quota <m>{Q_s}^\prime</m>, lower quota <m>{L_s}^\prime</m>, and upper quota <m>{U_s}^\prime</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title><term>Priority</term></title>

          <p>
            An order by which extra seats are assigned to states.
            An example is using the fractional part of the quota to determine which states get their upper quota of seats by ranking from largest fracitonal part to smallest.
          </p>
        </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </definition>

    <example>
      <title>Revisiting Coffee</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Taking each professor to be a state and each box of coffee to be a seat, we use the number of students each professor is teaching as the population (plus the professors themselves!).
