  <subsection xml:id="apportionment-problem-again">
    <title>The Apportionment Problem, but Mathier</title>

    <subsubsection xml:id="apportionment-variables-again">
      <title>Formal Mathematical Variables</title>

      <p>
        The problem can be defined using the following parameters:
      </p>
      <dl> <dt><m>M</m></dt> <dd> A positive integer representing the total number of items to be distributed (for example, the total number of seats in a legislative body). </dd> <dt><m>n</m></dt> <dd> The number of entities receiving the items (for example, states or political parties). </dd> <dt><m>\mathbf{p} = (p_1,\ldots,p_n)</m></dt> <dd> An <m>n</m>-dimensional real vector representing the size of each entity (such as population or vote counts). </dd> <dt><m>P</m></dt> <dd> The sum of all entries in <m>\mathbf{p}</m>, given by <m>P = \sum_{i=1}^n p_i</m>. </dd> <dt><m>\mathbf{m} = (m_1,\ldots,m_n)</m></dt> <dd> An integer vector where <m>m_s</m> is the final number of units allocated to entity <m>s</m>, satisfying <m>\sum_{s=1}^n m_s = M</m> and <m>m_s \ge 0</m>. </dd> </dl>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="exact-quota-again">
      <title>The (Exact) Quota</title>

      <p>
        A central concept in apportionment is the <term>exact quota</term>, denoted <m>q_s</m>, which represents the ideal fractional share of units an entity would receive if units were divisible.
        It is defined by
      </p>
      <me>
        q_s = \frac{p_s}{P} M.
      </me>

      <p>
        In an ideal mathematical world, we would have <m>m_s = q_s</m>.
        However, because <m>m_s</m> must be an integer and <m>q_s</m> is typically a rational number that is not an integer, an apportionment method is required to map the real vector of quotas to an integer vector of allocations.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <definition xml:id="definition-apportionment-method">
      <title>Apportionment Method: a function!</title>

      <p>
        Fix a positive integer <m>M</m> and an integer <m>n \ge 2</m>.
        Let <m>\mathcal{Q} = \{\mathbf{q} \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^n : \sum_{s=1}^n q_s = M\}</m> denote the space of exact quota vectors, and let <m>\mathcal{M} = \{\mathbf{m} \in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}^n : \sum_{s=1}^n m_s = M\}</m> denote the set of feasible integer allocations.
      </p>

      <p>
        An <term>apportionment method</term> is a function
      </p>
      <me>
        F : \mathcal{Q} \longrightarrow \mathcal{M}
      </me>

      <p>
        that assigns to each exact quota vector <m>\mathbf{q}</m> a unique integer allocation <m>\mathbf{m} = F(\mathbf{q})</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The method <m>F</m> induces a partition of <m>\mathcal{Q}</m> into regions
      </p>
      <me>
        P(\mathbf{m}) = \{\mathbf{q} \in Q : F(\mathbf{q}) = \mathbf{m}\},
      </me>

      <p>
        called the <term>apportionment polyhedra</term>.
        Each polyhedron consists of all exact quota vectors that are mapped to the same integer allocation.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <figure>
      <caption>
      <p>
        The positive orthant, <m>\mathcal{Q}</m>, and <m>\mathcal{M}</m>
      </p>
      with three states and five seats.
      </caption>
      <image scale="90%">
        <latex-image>
