  <subsection xml:id="apportionment-polyhedra">
    <title>Apportionment Polyhedra</title>

    <p>
      An <term>apportionment polyhedron</term> is a geometric region within the space of all possible exact quota vectors <m>\mathbf{q}</m> that a particular apportionment method maps to a single, fixed integer seat allocation <m>\mathbf{m}</m>.
      In mathematical terms, it consists of all fractional quota vectors that result in the same whole-number distribution of seats.
    </p>

    <subsubsection xml:id="polyhedron-geometry">
      <title>Geometric Representation</title>

      <p>
        The concept is best understood by visualizing the space of exact quotas.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <em>The set <m>\mathcal{Q}</m>.</em> This set contains all possible fractional distributions of <m>M</m> items among <m>n</m> entities. For example, if there are three parties and <m>M = 5</m> seats, the set <m>\mathcal{Q}</m> forms an equilateral triangle in three-dimensional space with vertices at <m>(5,0,0)</m>, <m>(0,5,0)</m>, and <m>(0,0,5)</m>.
        </li>

        <li>
          <em>Integer points.</em> Within this geometric space, the possible integer seat allocations are represented by discrete lattice points corresponding to integer vectors <m>\mathbf{m}</m>.
        </li>

        <li>
          <em>Defining the polyhedra.</em> A valid apportionment method partitions the larger region <m>Q</m> into smaller regions, or polyhedra, each associated with a single integer allocation. Any exact quota vector lying inside a given polyhedron is rounded or mapped to the integer allocation corresponding to that region.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The shape and size of apportionment polyhedra depend on the error function or rounding rule associated with the chosen apportionment method.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <em>Mapping.</em> A desirable property of an apportionment method is that any exact quota vector that is already an integer vector is mapped to itself.
        </li>

        <li>
          <em>Borders.</em> The boundaries between apportionment polyhedra correspond to tie situations, where an exact quota vector may be equally compatible with two or more integer seat allocations. In such cases, the method must include a rule for resolving ties along these boundaries.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        To find apportionment polyhedra given an apportionment function, it is useful to introduce <term>barycentric coordinates</term>.
        Given a value <m>M</m>, a barycentric <m>n</m>-tuple <m>(q_1,q_2,\cdots,q_m)</m> satisfies the property that <m>q_i \geq 0</m> for each coordinate and <m>\sum_{i = 1}^n q_i = M</m>.
        We can imagine each tuple with an <m>M</m> in one spot and zeros elsewhere to be sitting at the corner of an <m>n</m>-dimensional simplex, which results from treating the tuple like the end of a vector with tail at the original in <m>\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^n</m>.
        Given a coordinate <m>q_i</m>, the face opposite its is the set of all barycentric coordinates with a zero in position <m>i</m>.
      </p>

      <figure>
        <caption>
        <p>
          Moving around the triangle where the barycentric coordinates sum to five.
          The barycentric tuple <m>P = (2,2,1)</m> lies on the line <m>q_2 = 2</m> (and the line <m>q_1 = 2</m>, and the line <m>q_3 = 1</m>).
        </p>
        </caption>

        <image>
          <latex-image>
  \usetikzlibrary{calc}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1, line cap=round, line join=round]
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\N}{5} % sum of coordinates
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\S}{6} % side length (visual)
  % Equilateral triangle vertices
  \coordinate (A) at (0,0);                 % (N,0,0) i.e. q1=N
  \coordinate (B) at (\S,0);                % (0,N,0) i.e. q2=N
  \coordinate (C) at ({\S/2},{\S*sqrt(3)/2}); % (0,0,N) i.e. q3=N
  % Barycentric placement helper:
  % point with (q1,q2,q3) and q1+q2+q3 = N is
  % (q1/N)*A + (q2/N)*B + (q3/N)*C
  \newcommand{\Bary}[4]{%
    \pgfmathsetmacro{\wone}{#1/\N}%
    \pgfmathsetmacro{\wtwo}{#2/\N}%
    \pgfmathsetmacro{\wthree}{#3/\N}%
    \coordinate (#4) at ($ \wone*(A) + \wtwo*(B) + \wthree*(C) $);%
  }
  % Draw the simplex boundary
  \draw[thick] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- cycle;
  % Label vertices
  \node[below left]  at (A) {$(5,0,0)$};
  \node[below right] at (B) {$(0,5,0)$};
  \node[above]       at (C) {$(0,0,5)$};
  % (Optional) label axes directions on edges
  % \node[font=\small, below] at ($(A)!0.5!(B)$) {$q_3=0$};
  % \node[font=\small, left]  at ($(A)!0.5!(C)$) {$q_2=0$};
  % \node[font=\small, right] at ($(B)!0.5!(C)$) {$q_1=0$};
  % Boundary for q1 - q3 = 1 intersects at (1,4,0) and (3,0,2)
  \Bary{4}{1}{0}{P} % (4,1,0)
  \Bary{0}{1}{4}{Q} % (0,1,4)
  \Bary{0}{2}{3}{S} % (3,2,0)
  \Bary{3}{2}{0}{T}
  \Bary{2}{3}{0}{R} % (0,2,3)
  \Bary{0}{3}{2}{L} % (2,3,0)
  \Bary{1}{4}{0}{N} % (0,3,2)
  \Bary{0}{4}{1}{M} % (1,4,0)
  \Bary{2}{2}{1}{X} % (2,2,1)
  \Bary{1.5}{1.5}{2}{Y}
  % Draw the boundary line (dashed since strict inequality)
  \draw[thick, dashed] (P) -- (Q) node[midway, above] {$q_2 = 1$};
  \draw[thick, dashed] (S) -- (T) node[midway, above] {$q_2 = 2$};
  \draw[thick, dashed] (R) -- (L) node[midway, above] {$q_2 = 3$};
  \draw[thick, dashed] (M) -- (N) node[midway, above] {$q_2 = 4$};
  \fill (X) circle (1.2pt) node[above left, font=\small] {$P$};
  \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="apportionment-axioms-again">
      <title>Mathematical Constraints and Properties</title>

      <p>
        For an apportionment method to be considered fair or valid, it is often expected to satisfy certain mathematical axioms:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <term>Symmetry.</term> The outcome should not depend on the order in which the entities are listed.
        </li>

        <li>
          <term>Homogeneity.</term> If all input values <m>q_j</m> are multiplied by the same positive constant, the resulting apportionment should remain unchanged.
        </li>

        <li>
          <term>The Quota Rule.</term> A method satisfies this rule if each allocation <m>m_j</m> is either <m>\lfloor q_j \rfloor</m> or <m>\lceil q_j \rceil</m>.
        </li>

        <li>
          <term>House Monotonicity.</term> If the total number of units increases from <m>M</m> to <m>M+1</m>, no entity's allocation <m>m_j</m> should decrease. This property rules out paradoxes such as the Alabama Paradox.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The Balinski-Young Theorem essentially says that there is no apportionment rule that satisfies all of these criteria all the time!
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="example-hamilton-method">
      <title>Hamilton's Method as an Apportionment Function</title>

      <p>
        Fix <m>M</m> seats and <m>n</m> entities.
        Given an exact quota vector <m>\mathbf{q} \in \mathcal{Q}</m>, Hamilton's method proceeds as follows:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          Assign each entity its lower quota <m>m_j = \lfloor q_j \rfloor</m>.
        </li>

        <li>
          Distribute the remaining seats, one at a time, to the entities with the largest fractional remainders <m>q_j - \lfloor q_j \rfloor</m>.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        This defines an apportionment method <m>F_{\text{Ham}} : \mathcal{Q} \to \mathcal{M}</m>, since every quota vector is mapped to a unique integer allocation.
      </p>

      <p>
        Geometrically, the apportionment polyhedra for Hamilton's method are regions determined by inequalities comparing fractional parts of the quotas.
        The boundaries between polyhedra occur where two or more remainders are equal, leading to ties in the final seat assignment.
      </p>

      <p>
        Because these boundaries depend on global comparisons of remainders, the resulting polyhedra need not arise from minimizing a single global error function.
        This explains why Hamilton's method can satisfy the Quota Rule while still exhibiting paradoxes such as the Alabama Paradox.
      </p>

      <example xml:id="example-hamilton-polyhedron-coffee-3-6-6">
        <title>The Hamilton Polyhedron for an example.</title>

        <p>
          Suppose we have <m>M=15</m> boxes of coffee to distribute among three people (Ahmed, Gibbons, and Tripp) in proportion to their <q>need scores</q> <m>\mathbf{a}=(35,55,58)</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          The total is <m>A = 35+55+58 = 148</m>, so the exact quotas are <m>q_j = \dfrac{a_j}{A}M</m>.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table-coffee-quotas">
          <title>Exact quotas and remainders for 15 boxes</title>

          <tabular>
            <col align="left"/> <col align="right"/> <col align="right"/> <col align="right"/> <col align="right"/>
            <row header="yes">
              <cell>Person</cell>
              <cell><m>a_j</m></cell>
              <cell><m>q_j=\frac{a_j}{148}\cdot 15</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\lfloor q_j\rfloor</m></cell>
              <cell>Remainder</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Ahmed</cell>
              <cell><m>35</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\frac{525}{148}\approx 3.547</m></cell>
              <cell><m>3</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\approx 0.547</m></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Gibbons</cell>
              <cell><m>55</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\frac{825}{148}\approx 5.574</m></cell>
              <cell><m>5</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\approx 0.574</m></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Tripp</cell>
              <cell><m>58</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\frac{870}{148}\approx 5.878</m></cell>
              <cell><m>5</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\approx 0.878</m></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
          Using Hamilton's (largest remainder) method, we first assign lower quotas: <m>(3,5,5)</m>, which totals <m>13</m> boxes, leaving <m>2</m> boxes to distribute.
          The two largest remainders are Tripp's and Gibbons's, so each receives one additional box.
        </p>

        <p>
          The final allocation is
          <me>
            (m_{\text{Ahmed}},m_{\text{Gibbons}},m_{\text{Tripp}}) = (3,6,6).
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          Consider Hamilton’s method on the quota simplex <m>\mathcal{Q}=\{(q_A,q_G,q_T)\in\mathbb{R}_{\ge0}^3:\ q_A+q_G+q_T=15\}</m>.
          We describe the region of quota vectors that Hamilton maps to the allocation <m>(3,6,6)</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          For Hamilton’s method to output <m>(3,6,6)</m>, the lower-quota step must first produce
          <ul>
            <li>
              Two seats left: <m>(2,5,6)</m>, <m>(2,6,5)</m>, or <m>(3,5,5)</m>;
            </li>

            <li>
              One seat left: <m>(2,6,6)</m>, <m>(3,5,6)</m>, or <m>(3,6,5)</m>;
            </li>

            <li>
              No seats left: <m>(3,6,6)</m>.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
          In our example, we have the scenario that the lower quotas produce <m>(3,5,5)</m>, and the remainders satisfy <m>r_G \gt r_A</m>, <m>r_T > r_A</m>:
        </p>
        <me>
          3 \le q_A &lt; 4,\qquad 5 \le q_G &lt; 6,\qquad 5 \le q_T &lt; 6, \qquad q_A+q_G+q_T=15.
        </me>

        <p>
          Write the remainders as <m>r_A=q_A-3</m>, <m>r_G=q_G-5</m>, and <m>r_T=q_T-5</m>.
          To award the two remaining seats to Gibbons and Tripp (and not to Ahmed), we need the two inequalities
        </p>
        <me>
          r_G &gt; r_A,\qquad r_T &gt; r_A.
        </me>

        <p>
          Equivalently, these are linear conditions on the quotas:
        </p>
        <me>
          q_G - q_A &gt; 2,\qquad q_T - q_A &gt; 2.
        </me>

        <p>
          Therefore, part of the (open) Hamilton region mapping to <m>(3,6,6)</m> is
        </p>
        <me>
          P(3,6,6) \supseteq \left\{ (q_A,q_G,q_T)\in Q: \begin{array}{l} 3 \le q_A &lt; 4,\ 5 \le q_G &lt; 6,\ 5 \le q_T &lt; 6,\\ q_G-q_A &gt; 2,\ q_T-q_A &gt; 2 \end{array} \right\}.
        </me>

        <p>
          The boundary of this region consists of tie situations, such as <m>r_G=r_A</m> or <m>r_T=r_A</m>, together with the grid oundaries where a quota crosses an integer and a lower quota changes (for example, <m>q_G=6</m>).
          A complete closed polyhedron requires a specified tie-breaking rule on these boundary sets.
        </p>
      </example>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="apportionment-polyhedra-again">
    <title>Apportionment Polyhedra</title>

    <p>
      An <term>apportionment polyhedron</term> is a geometric region within the space of all possible exact quota vectors <m>\mathbf{q}</m> that a particular apportionment method maps to a single, fixed integer seat allocation <m>\mathbf{m}</m>.
      In mathematical terms, it consists of all fractional quota vectors that result in the same whole-number distribution of seats.
    </p>

    <subsubsection xml:id="polyhedron-geometry-again">
      <title>Geometric Representation</title>

      <p>
        The concept is best understood by visualizing the space of exact quotas.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <em>The set <m>Q</m>.</em> This set contains all possible fractional distributions of <m>M</m> items among <m>n</m> entities. For example, if there are three parties and <m>M = 5</m> seats, the set <m>Q</m> forms an equilateral triangle in three-dimensional space with vertices at <m>(5,0,0)</m>, <m>(0,5,0)</m>, and <m>(0,0,5)</m>.
        </li>

        <li>
          <em>Integer points.</em> Within this geometric space, the possible integer seat allocations are represented by discrete lattice points corresponding to integer vectors <m>\mathbf{m}</m>.
        </li>

        <li>
          <em>Defining the polyhedra.</em> A valid apportionment method partitions the larger region <m>Q</m> into smaller regions, or polyhedra, each associated with a single integer allocation. Any exact quota vector lying inside a given polyhedron is rounded or mapped to the integer allocation corresponding to that region.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="polyhedron-function">
      <title>Function and Boundaries</title>

      <p>
        The shape and size of apportionment polyhedra depend on the error function or rounding rule associated with the chosen apportionment method.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <em>Mapping.</em> A desirable property of an apportionment method is that any exact quota vector that is already an integer vector is mapped to itself.
        </li>

        <li>
          <em>Borders.</em> The boundaries between apportionment polyhedra correspond to tie situations, where an exact quota vector may be equally compatible with two or more integer seat allocations. In such cases, the method must include a rule for resolving ties along these boundaries.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="polyhedra-fairness">
    <title>Using Apportionment Polyhedra to Assess Fairness</title>

    <p>
      The <term>apportionment polyhedron</term> provides a geometric framework for assessing the fairness of an apportionment method by describing how the method rounds exact quotas—the ideal fractional shares of seats—into whole-number allocations.
      By analyzing the shapes and boundaries of these polyhedra, mathematicians and social scientists can determine which methods minimize rounding error and which are susceptible to representational paradoxes.
    </p>

    <subsubsection xml:id="visualizing-rounding-error">
      <title>Visualizing Rounding and Error</title>

      <p>
        Geometrically, the space of all possible exact quota vectors <m>Q</m> is partitioned into regions, or polyhedra, each associated with a single integer seat allocation.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <em>Mapping fairness.</em> A well-behaved apportionment method maps any exact quota vector lying inside a given polyhedron to the integer allocation at its center. Fairness can then be assessed by measuring the <term>gravity of the error</term>, defined as the distance or discrepancy between the ideal quota <m>q_j</m> and the resulting integer allocation <m>m_j</m>.
        </li>

        <li>
          <em>Defining boundaries.</em> The boundaries between polyhedra correspond to tie situations, in which an exact quota vector may be equally compatible with more than one integer allocation. A fair method must resolve such ties using a consistent rule, rather than an arbitrary choice.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="comparing-methods">
      <title>Comparing Methodological Justness</title>

      <p>
        Different apportionment methods correspond to different error functions, and these choices lead to distinct polyhedral decompositions of the quota space.
        This geometric perspective allows for direct mathematical comparison between methods.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <em>Sainte-Lague versus Hare.</em> Different methods induce different polyhedral grids. The Sainte&#8209;Laguë method is designed to minimize relative error and, in three-entity settings, produces a hexagonal tiling that is often regarded as neutral with respect to entity size. In contrast, rounding methods such as Hare’s method focus on greatest remainders and are frequently analyzed using <m>\ell_p</m>-norms—such as Euclidean distance—to identify the integer point closest to a given exact quota.
        </li>

        <li>
          <em>Bias detection.</em> Geometric analysis can reveal systematic biases in an apportionment method. For example, linear divisor methods may favor smaller entities or larger entities depending on the placement of their polyhedral boundaries, often parametrized by an initial divisor offset.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="paradoxes">
      <title>Identifying Paradoxes</title>

      <p>
        Apportionment polyhedra are also a useful tool for determining whether a method satisfies common axioms of fairness.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <em>The Quota Rule.</em> Polyhedral analysis can be used to check whether allocations always lie between the floor and ceiling of the exact quotas.
        </li>

        <li>
          <em>House monotonicity.</em> Examining how polyhedral boundaries change as the total number of seats <m>M</m> increases helps determine whether a method avoids decreases in allocation when additional seats are added.
        </li>

        <li>
          <em>The Alabama Paradox.</em> Poorly aligned polyhedral boundaries can lead to paradoxical outcomes in which increasing the total number of seats causes an entity to lose a seat, a phenomenon observed in historical legislative apportionments.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        A contemporary illustration of these issues arose in the 2020 Iowa Democratic caucuses.
        The use of Hamilton's method, which satisfies the Quota Rule but is geometrically vulnerable to paradoxes, produced several aggregation anomalies in which the sum of local allocations failed to align with statewide totals.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="affine-simplices-barycentric">
    <title>Affine Sets, Simplices, and Barycentric Coordinates</title>

    <p>
      These notes assume familiarity with vectors, matrices, linear independence, and basic multivariable geometry.
    </p>

    <p>
      In linear algebra, a subspace must contain the origin and be closed under linear combinations.
      Many geometric objects we care about are shifted versions of subspaces.
    </p>

    <p>
      This leads to the idea of an <term>affine set</term>.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="affine-combination">
      <title>Affine Combination</title>

      <p>
        A vector of the form
      </p>
      <me>
        \lambda_1 v_1 + \lambda_2 v_2 + \cdots + \lambda_k v_k
      </me>

      <p>
        is called an <term>affine combination</term> of <m>v_1,\dots,v_k</m> if
      </p>
      <me>
        \lambda_1+\lambda_2+\cdots+\lambda_k = 1.
      </me>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="affine-hull">
      <title>Affine Hull</title>

      <p>
        The <term>affine hull</term> of points <m>v_1,\dots,v_k</m> is the set
      </p>
      <me>
        \operatorname{aff}(v_1,\dots,v_k) = \left\{ \sum_{i=1}^k \lambda_i v_i : \sum_{i=1}^k \lambda_i = 1 \right\}.
      </me>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="affine-hull-example">
      <p>
        Two distinct points determine a line, three noncollinear points determine a plane, and four noncoplanar points determine a three-dimensional affine space.
      </p>
    </example>

    <definition xml:id="affine-independence">
      <title>Affine Independence</title>

      <p>
        Points <m>v_0,\dots,v_n</m> are <term>affinely independent</term> if the vectors
        <me>
          v_1-v_0,\; v_2-v_0,\; \dots,\; v_n-v_0
        </me>
        are linearly independent.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="convex-set">
      <title>Convex Set</title>

      <p>
        A set <m>C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n</m> is <term>convex</term> if for any <m>x,y \in C</m>,
      </p>
      <me>
        (1-t)x+ty \in C \quad\text{for all }0\le t\le 1.
      </me>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="convex-combination">
      <title>Convex Combination</title>

      <p>
        A <term>convex combination</term> is an affine combination where all coefficients are nonnegative:
      </p>
      <me>
        \lambda_i \ge 0, \qquad \sum_{i=1}^k \lambda_i = 1.
      </me>
    </definition>

    <p>
      A simplex is the higher-dimensional analogue of a triangle or tetrahedron.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="simplex-definition">
      <title>Simplex</title>

      <p>
        If <m>v_0,\dots,v_n</m> are affinely independent points in <m>\mathbb{R}^m</m>, then the simplex they determine is
      </p>
      <me>
        \operatorname{conv}(v_0,\dots,v_n) = \left\{ \sum_{i=0}^n \lambda_i v_i : \lambda_i \ge 0, \; \sum_{i=0}^n \lambda_i = 1 \right\}.
      </me>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="simplex-examples">
      <ol marker="1">
        <li>
          <p>
            A line segment is a <m>1</m>-simplex.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            A triangle is a <m>2</m>-simplex.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            A tetrahedron is a <m>3</m>-simplex.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </example>

    <definition xml:id="standard-simplex">
      <title>Standard Simplex</title>

      <p>
        The <term>standard <m>n</m>-simplex</term> is
      </p>
      <me>
        \Delta_n = \left\{ (x_0,\dots,x_n)\in\mathbb{R}^{n+1} : x_i\ge0, \; \sum_{i=0}^n x_i = 1 \right\}.
      </me>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Barycentric coordinates express a point relative to the vertices of a simplex.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="barycentric-definition">
      <title>Barycentric Coordinates</title>

      <p>
        Suppose <m>v_0,\dots,v_n</m> are affinely independent.
        Every point <m>p</m> in the affine hull can be written uniquely as
      </p>
      <me>
        p = \lambda_0 v_0 + \lambda_1 v_1 + \cdots + \lambda_n v_n
      </me>

      <p>
        with
      </p>
      <me>
        \lambda_0+\cdots+\lambda_n=1.
      </me>

      <p>
        The numbers <m>\lambda_0,\dots,\lambda_n</m> are called the barycentric coordinates of <m>p</m>.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <p>
      If all barycentric coordinates are positive, then the point lies in the interior of the simplex.
      If some coordinate is zero, then the point lies on a face of the simplex.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="barycentric-example">
      <p>
        Let
      </p>
      <me>
        v_0=(0,0), \qquad v_1=(1,0), \qquad v_2=(0,1).
      </me>

      <p>
        Suppose
      </p>
      <me>
        p=(0.2,0.3).
      </me>

      <p>
        Then
      </p>
      <me>
        p = 0.5v_0 + 0.2v_1 + 0.3v_2.
      </me>

      <p>
        Since the coefficients are nonnegative and sum to <m>1</m>, the point lies inside the triangle.
      </p>
    </example>

    <p>
      To compute barycentric coordinates, solve the system
    </p>
    <me>
      p = \sum_{i=0}^n \lambda_i v_i
    </me>

    <p>
      together with
    </p>
    <me>
      \lambda_0+\cdots+\lambda_n=1.
    </me>

    <p>
      A convenient matrix form is
    </p>
    <me>
      \begin{bmatrix} &amp; &amp; \\ v_0 &amp; v_1 &amp; \cdots v_n \\ &amp; &amp; \\ 1 &amp; 1 &amp; \cdots 1 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} \lambda_0\\ \lambda_1\\ \vdots\\ \lambda_n \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} p\\ 1 \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      Affine independence guarantees a unique solution.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <exercises xml:id="affine-simplex-exercises">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="affine-combination-check">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine whether each expression is an affine combination of the given vectors.
        </p>

        <ol marker="a">
          <li>
            <me>
              2v_1 - v_2
            </me>
          </li>

          <li>
            <me>
              \frac12 v_1 + \frac12 v_2
            </me>
          </li>

          <li>
            <me>
              3v_1 + 2v_2 - 4v_3
            </me>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="convex-combination-check">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine whether each expression is a convex combination.
        </p>

        <ol marker="a">
          <li>
            <me>
              \frac14 v_1 + \frac34 v_2
            </me>
          </li>

          <li>
            <me>
              2v_1 - v_2
            </me>
          </li>

          <li>
            <me>
              \frac13 v_1 + \frac13 v_2 + \frac13 v_3
            </me>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="affine-independence-check">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine whether the following points are affinely independent:
        </p>
        <me>
          (0,0),\quad (1,2),\quad (3,1).
        </me>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="triangle-barycentric">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
        </p>
        <me>
          v_0=(0,0), \qquad v_1=(2,0), \qquad v_2=(0,2).
        </me>

        <p>
          Find barycentric coordinates for the point
        </p>
        <me>
          p=(1,0.5).
        </me>

        <p>
          Is the point inside the simplex?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="standard-simplex-exercise">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Describe geometrically the standard simplices <m>\Delta_1</m>, <m>\Delta_2</m>, and <m>\Delta_3</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="matrix-barycentric">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
        </p>
        <me>
          v_0=(1,0), \qquad v_1=(3,1), \qquad v_2=(2,4).
        </me>

        <p>
          Find barycentric coordinates for
        </p>
        <me>
          p=(2,2).
        </me>

        <p>
          Write the corresponding augmented matrix system.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    </exercises>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="barycentric-compatible-functions">
    <title>Barycentric-Compatible Functions</title>

    <p>
      Barycentric coordinates are especially useful because they allow us to interpolate between objects.
      In many applications, we have two functions that assign points in a simplex, and we would like to move continuously between them.
    </p>

    <p>
      Suppose
    </p>
    <me>
      f,g : X \to \mathbb{R}^n
    </me>

    <p>
      are functions whose outputs lie in a convex set, such as a simplex.
      For any parameter <m>0 \le t \le 1</m>, define a new function
    </p>
    <me>
      h_t(x) = (1-t)f(x) + tg(x).
    </me>

    <p>
      Since the coefficients <m>1-t</m> and <m>t</m> are nonnegative and sum to <m>1</m>, the point <m>h_t(x)</m> is a convex combination of <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Geometrically, for each fixed <m>x</m>, the point <m>h_t(x)</m> travels along the line segment joining <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m>.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="barycentric-compatible-functions-def">
      <title>Barycentric-Compatible Functions</title>

      <p>
        Two functions
      </p>
      <me>
        f,g : X \to \Delta_n
      </me>

      <p>
        are called <term>barycentrically compatible</term> if for every <m>x \in X</m> and every <m>0 \le t \le 1</m>,
      </p>
      <me>
        (1-t)f(x) + tg(x)
      </me>

      <p>
        also lies in <m>\Delta_n</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Equivalently, the line segment joining <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> remains entirely inside the simplex.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <remark xml:id="convexity-remark">
      <p>
        Because simplices are convex sets, any two functions whose values lie in the same simplex are automatically barycentrically compatible.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <example xml:id="probability-example">
      <title>Probability Distribution Example</title>

      <p>
        Let
      </p>
      <me>
        \Delta_2 = \left\{ (x,y,z)\in\mathbb{R}^3 : x+y+z=1,\; x,y,z \ge 0 \right\}.
      </me>

      <p>
        A point in <m>\Delta_2</m> can be interpreted as a probability distribution on three outcomes.
      </p>

      <p>
        Suppose
      </p>
      <me>
        f(x) = (0.7,0.2,0.1), \qquad g(x) = (0.1,0.3,0.6).
      </me>

      <p>
        Then
      </p>
      <me>
        h_{1/2}(x) = \frac12 f(x) + \frac12 g(x) = (0.4,0.25,0.35).
      </me>

      <p>
        This point is still in the simplex because its coordinates remain nonnegative and sum to <m>1</m>.
      </p>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="geometric-simplex-path">
      <title>Paths Inside a Simplex</title>

      <p>
        Let <m>f,g : [0,1]\to\Delta_2</m>.
        For each input value <m>x</m>, the interpolation
      </p>
      <me>
        h_t(x) = (1-t)f(x)+tg(x)
      </me>

      <p>
        determines a path inside the simplex connecting the two images.
      </p>

      <p>
        As <m>t</m> varies from <m>0</m> to <m>1</m>, the point moves continuously from <m>f(x)</m> to <m>g(x)</m>.
      </p>
    </example>

    <exercise xml:id="barycentric-interpolation-exercise">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
        </p>
        <me>
          f(x) = (0.2,0.5,0.3), \qquad g(x) = (0.6,0.1,0.3).
        </me>

        <p>
          Compute
        </p>
        <me>
          h_{1/4}(x) = \frac34 f(x) + \frac14 g(x).
        </me>

        <p>
          Verify that the result lies in <m>\Delta_2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="simplex-line-segment">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain why the line segment between any two points in a simplex remains entirely inside the simplex.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="probability-interpolation">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose two probability distributions are given by
        </p>
        <me>
          p=(0.8,0.1,0.1), \qquad q=(0.2,0.5,0.3).
        </me>

        <p>
          Compute the halfway distribution
        </p>
        <me>
          \frac12 p + \frac12 q.
        </me>

        <p>
          Interpret the result geometrically inside the simplex.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="solving-linear-functionals">
    <title>A Practical Guide to Solving Systems of Linear Functionals</title>

    <p>
      Suppose we have several linear functionals
    </p>
    <me>
      \varphi_1,\varphi_2,\dots,\varphi_m : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}.
    </me>

    <p>
      A system of linear functionals asks us to find all vectors satisfying equations or inequalities involving these maps.
    </p>

    <p>
      Geometrically, such systems describe intersections of hyperplanes and half-spaces.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="functionals-dot-products">
    <title>Writing Functionals as Dot Products</title>

    <p>
      Every linear functional on <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m> can be written as a dot product
    </p>
    <me>
      \varphi(x)=a\cdot x
    </me>

    <p>
      for some vector <m>a\in\mathbb{R}^n</m>.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="dot-product-functional-example">
      <p>
        The functional
      </p>
      <me>
        \varphi(x,y,z)=2x-y+3z
      </me>

      <p>
        corresponds to the coefficient vector
      </p>
      <me>
        (2,-1,3).
      </me>

      <p>
        In matrix form,
      </p>
      <me>
        \varphi(x,y,z) = \begin{bmatrix} 2 &amp; -1 &amp; 3 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} x\\ y\\ z \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </example>
    <dl xml:id="matrix-form-step">
    <li>
      <title>Step 1: Convert to Matrix Form</title>

      <p>
        Suppose we want to solve
      </p>
      <me>
        \varphi_1(x,y,z)=1,
      </me>
      <me>
        \varphi_2(x,y,z)=4,
      </me>

      <p>
        where
      </p>
      <me>
        \varphi_1(x,y,z)=x+y+z,
      </me>
      <me>
        \varphi_2(x,y,z)=2x-y+z.
      </me>

      <p>
        Place the coefficient vectors into a matrix:
      </p>
      <me>
        A= \begin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 1 &amp; 1\\ 2 &amp; -1 &amp; 1 \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>

      <p>
        Then solve the matrix equation
      </p>
      <me>
        A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b},
      </me>

      <p>
        where
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{x}= \begin{bmatrix} x\\ y\\ z \end{bmatrix}, \qquad \mathbf{b}= \begin{bmatrix} 1\\ 4 \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </li>

    <li>
      <title>Step 2: Row Reduce</title>

      <p>
        Form the augmented matrix
      </p>
      <me>
        \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 &amp; 1 &amp; 1 &amp; 1\\ 2 &amp; -1 &amp; 1 &amp; 4 \end{array} \right].
      </me>

      <p>
        Perform the row operation
      </p>
      <me>
        R_2 \to R_2 - 2R_1.
      </me>

      <p>
        This gives
      </p>
      <me>
        \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 &amp; 1 &amp; 1 &amp; 1\\ 0 &amp; -3 &amp; -1 &amp; 2 \end{array} \right].
      </me>

      <p>
        The second equation becomes
      </p>
      <me>
        -3y-z=2.
      </me>

      <p>
        Let <m>z=t</m>.
        Then
      </p>
      <me>
        y=-\frac{2+t}{3}.
      </me>

      <p>
        Substitute into the first equation:
      </p>
      <me>
        x+y+z=1.
      </me>

      <p>
        Solving for <m>x</m> gives
      </p>
      <me>
        x=\frac{5-2t}{3}.
      </me>

      <p>
        The solution set is
      </p>
      <me>
        \left( \frac{5-2t}{3}, -\frac{2+t}{3}, t \right).
      </me>
    </li>

    <li>
      <title>Geometric Interpretation</title>

      <p>
        Each equation
      </p>
      <me>
        \varphi(x)=b
      </me>

      <p>
        defines a hyperplane.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            One equation in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m> defines a plane.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Two independent equations typically intersect in a line.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Three independent equations typically intersect in a point.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>

    <li>
      <title>Inequalities and Half-Spaces</title>

      <p>
        Conditions such as
      </p>
      <me>
        \varphi(x)\ge c
      </me>

      <p>
        describe half-spaces.
      </p>

      <example xml:id="half-space-example">
        <p>
          The inequality
        </p>
        <me>
          x+y+z \le 1
        </me>

        <p>
          cuts space into two regions separated by the plane
        </p>
        <me>
          x+y+z=1.
        </me>
      </example>

      <p>
        Systems of inequalities therefore describe convex regions.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <title>Systems Inside a Simplex</title>

      <p>
        Suppose we work in the standard simplex
      </p>
      <me>
        \Delta_2 = \left\{ (x,y,z): x+y+z=1, \; x,y,z\ge0 \right\}.
      </me>

      <p>
        Now add the inequalities
      </p>
      <me>
        4x\ge1, \qquad 4y&lt;2.
      </me>

      <p>
        Dividing by <m>4</m> gives
      </p>
      <me>
        x\ge\frac14, \qquad y&lt;\frac12.
      </me>

      <p>
        These inequalities carve out a smaller region inside the simplex.
      </p>
    </li>
    </dl>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="allocation-strategy">
    <title>Strategy for Allocation Problems</title>

    <p>
      In allocation problems, systems often involve:
    </p>

    <ol marker="1">
      <li>
        <p>
          The simplex equation
        </p>
        <me>
          x_1+\cdots+x_n=1.
        </me>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Nonnegativity conditions
        </p>
        <me>
          x_i\ge0.
        </me>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Floor constraints
        </p>
        <me>
          m_i \le Mx_i &lt; m_i+1.
        </me>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Remainder comparisons
        </p>
        <me>
          Mx_i-\lfloor Mx_i\rfloor \ge Mx_j-\lfloor Mx_j\rfloor.
        </me>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="workflow">
    <title>Practical Workflow</title>

    <subsubsection xml:id="workflow-affine">
      <title>Step A: Solve the Affine Constraints</title>

      <p>
        Usually begin with the equation
      </p>
      <me>
        x_1+\cdots+x_n=1.
      </me>

      <p>
        This reduces the dimension by one.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="workflow-slabs">
      <title>Step B: Add Interval Constraints</title>

      <p>
        Suppose an allocation requires
      </p>
      <me>
        m_i \le Mx_i &lt; m_i+1.
      </me>

      <p>
        Dividing by <m>M</m> gives
      </p>
      <me>
        \frac{m_i}{M} \le x_i &lt; \frac{m_i+1}{M}.
      </me>

      <p>
        These inequalities define slabs bounded by parallel hyperplanes.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="workflow-remainders">
      <title>Step C: Add Remainder Comparisons</title>

      <p>
        Compare the fractional parts of the quota coordinates.
      </p>

      <example xml:id="remainder-linearization">
        <p>
          Suppose
        </p>
        <me>
          \lfloor4x\rfloor=1, \qquad \lfloor4y\rfloor=0.
        </me>

        <p>
          Then the inequality
        </p>
        <me>
          4x-\lfloor4x\rfloor \ge 4y-\lfloor4y\rfloor
        </me>

        <p>
          becomes
        </p>
        <me>
          4x-1 \ge 4y.
        </me>

        <p>
          Rearranging gives
        </p>
        <me>
          x-y\ge\frac14.
        </me>

        <p>
          Inside a fixed slab region, the floor functions become constants, so the boundaries become linear.
        </p>
      </example>
    </subsubsection>

    <p>
      Systems of linear functionals partition space into regions bounded by hyperplanes.
    </p>

    <p>
      Inside each region:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          the inequalities are consistent,
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          the allocation rule is constant,
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          and the geometry is convex.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      This viewpoint turns allocation methods into geometric partitions of simplices.
    </p>

    <exercises xml:id="linear-functional-exercises">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="convert-functional-exercise">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write each linear functional as a row vector.
        </p>

        <ol marker="a">
          <li>
            <me>
              \varphi(x,y)=3x-2y
            </me>
          </li>

          <li>
            <me>
              \psi(x,y,z)=x+4y-z
            </me>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="solve-functional-system">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the system
        </p>
        <me>
          x+y+z=2,
        </me>
        <me>
          2x-y+z=1.
        </me>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="simplex-region-exercise">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Describe geometrically the set of points in <m>\Delta_2</m> satisfying
        </p>
        <me>
          x\ge\frac13.
        </me>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="allocation-slabs-exercise">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>M=5</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Rewrite the constraints
        </p>
        <me>
          2 \le 5x &lt; 3
        </me>

        <p>
          as inequalities involving only <m>x</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="remainder-comparison-exercise">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose
        </p>
        <me>
          \lfloor6x\rfloor=2, \qquad \lfloor6y\rfloor=1.
        </me>

        <p>
          Simplify the inequality
        </p>
        <me>
          6x-\lfloor6x\rfloor \ge 6y-\lfloor6y\rfloor.
        </me>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    </exercises>
  </subsection>
</section>